[
    {
        /*
        Registers the hibernate filter that scopes billing data to the locations the authenticated user
        is allowed. Which locations those are is resolved by the allowedlocation module, from the
        allowed_location user property it also scopes the login location picker with, so that one user
        property drives both; see BillingLocationFilterListener, which sets the parameter below, and the
        allowedlocation.* global properties for how the resolution is configured. The filter does
        nothing at all until billing.dataFilter.enabled is set to true.

        datafilter discovers this file across every module's classpath (classpath*:/filters/hibernate/
        *.json using OpenmrsClassLoader), the same way it discovers its own built-in registrations, so no
        changes to the datafilter module itself are needed for this to take effect. What datafilter does
        with this registration is enable the filter on each session and let the listener set its
        parameter. The conditions live with the mappings, see below.

        This filter does NOT read datafilter_entity_basis_map, so it needs no per-user grant rows in that
        table, and it is independent of datafilter's own location based filters, which scope patients and
        their clinical data rather than any of the billing tables.

        Why "targetClasses" is empty and there is no "condition":

            targetClasses tells datafilter which classes to add the filter to, and there are none to add
            it to here: every class this filter applies to already declares it, with the condition for
            its own table, either in Bill.hbm.xml and Cashier.hbm.xml (where the filter-def also lives)
            or with a @Filter annotation, on BillDiscount and BillRefund. A single condition here could
            not serve them anyway, since each table reaches org.openmrs.Location differently: a bill
            through the cash point it was created at, a catalogue entry (BillableService, BillableDrug)
            through its own nullable location_id, and the rest through one of those two.

            Listing the xml mapped classes here would actively break the server. datafilter can only add
            filters to a module's mapping file as a whole: it matches the file by its FIRST <class>
            element (Util.getMappedClassName) and then applies one condition to every class in it. So
            naming CashPoint, the first class of Cashier.hbm.xml, would have it rewrite that file and add
            a second, condition-less filter tag to both CashPoint and Timesheet, and a filter with no
            condition makes hibernate fail to build the session factory (NullPointerException while
            instantiating the entity persister). Naming Bill, BillLineItem or Payment would silently do
            nothing instead, since Bill.hbm.xml starts with CashierItemPrice.

        Parameters:
            allowedLocationIds: the location ids resolved from the user property, plus their descendants
                                unless allowedlocation.includeDescendants is false. Never empty, an
                                unmatchable id is used when the user is allowed nothing so that the
                                filter fails closed.

        Catalogue rows whose location_id is null are global and stay visible to everyone, which is how
        the searches in HibernateBillableServiceDAOImpl and HibernateBillableDrugDAOImpl treat them and
        what the billing strategies fall back to when a location has no entry of its own.
        */
        "name": "billing_locationBasedBillingFilter",
        "targetClasses": [],
        "parameters": [
            {
                "name": "allowedLocationIds",
                "type": "integer"
            }
        ]
    }
]
